<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">June 1381 · Blackheath, near London</p><h1 class="sp-title">John Ball at Blackheath</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ball">John Ball</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>In 1381 a third poll tax in four years set off a rising in Essex and Kent. As the rebels gathered on Blackheath outside London, John Ball, a priest who had been imprisoned again and again for his preaching, gave them an open-air sermon on the equality of all people before God.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>When Adam delved and Eve span, who was then the gentleman?</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">The couplet on which he preached, as reported by the chronicler Thomas Walsingham.</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“When Adam delved and Eve span, who was then the gentleman?” — John Ball, John Ball at Blackheath" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-john-ball-at-blackheath.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The rebels entered London days later, and their leader Wat Tyler was killed while parleying with the fourteen-year-old Richard II at Smithfield. Ball was captured and executed at St Albans in July. The rising was crushed, but no further poll tax was levied, and serfdom in England faded away over the following century.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peasants-revolt">Peasants' Revolt</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a></div></div></section>
